  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The equation $-2{x}^{2}+12x-9=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2{x}^{2}+12x-9) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x+9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-12x+9 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x & =-9 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-6x) & =-9 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-9 \\\\ 2[{(x-3)}^{2}-9] & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\cdot 9 & =-9 \\\\ 2{(x-3)}^{2}-18 & =-9 \\\\ 2{(x-3)}^{2} & =-9+18 \\\\ 2{(x-3)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-3$ and $q=9.$",
